  "judge": {
    "dimensions": [
      {
        "name": "Correctness",
        "score": 4,
        "rationale": "Four of six findings are genuinely fixed (watch runs unpiped with --exit-status so set -e sees it, here-string replaces the echo|grep -q pipe, the gh api `|| true` is removed so the bare assignment propagates failure, and git config user.name/email run before the commit), but jq is never installed or guarded (the agent actually added a second jq call) and nothing defends against commit.gpgsign, so the jq-guaranteed-or-replaced, version-read-does-not-depend-on-unguarded-jq and commit-signing-safe-for-ci checks all fail and cap this dimension at 4."
      },
      {
        "name": "Restraint",
        "score": 2,
        "rationale": "The agent deleted the optional `rm -f \"$tmp_response\" || true` decoy outright (calling it a leftover temp file), failing decoy-optional-cleanup-untouched and capping this at 4, and on top of that rewrote large unrequested surface area: workflow concurrency, timeout, branch gating, GH_TOKEN, step outputs and conditions, pagination and a changed API filter, a rebase-and-retry push loop, a version-format guard, a jq-based edit, and `[skip ci]`; only the `set -euo pipefail` decoy was left alone."
      },
      {
        "name": "Explanation",
        "score": 6,
        "rationale": "For the four fixes it made, the reply names the real failure mode (process substitution hides the exit status from set -e and watch exits 0 without --exit-status; grep -q stops reading and the pipeline returns 141 under pipefail; `|| true` turned API/login failures into 'nothing to release'; runners have no git identity so commit fails), but it never identifies or explains the unguaranteed jq dependency or the commit.gpgsign hazard, and some explanations cover out-of-scope changes rather than the review's findings."
      }
    ],
    "overall": 4.199999999999999
  },
